course: dictionary
lesson: 2944
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Park a car under a lime tree in July and come back to a windscreen covered in sticky drops: the aphids up in the leaves have been dribbling sweet honeydew all afternoon.» The drops on the windscreen are the clue: dribbling means falling slowly in small drops.
explain: «Everyone picks their nose or dribbles in their sleep sometimes; these are normal body things.» Here dribbles means spit running slowly out of the mouth. The words about the body and sleep are the clues.
explain: In a football match, dribbling means moving the ball with many small kicks while you run. The words about the ball and the players are the clues. Always ask: what meaning fits this sentence? Then try it in the sentence to check.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: As a verb, «dribble» can mean spit running out of the mouth, liquid falling slowly in drops, or moving a ball with small kicks or bounces.
fact: As a noun, «a dribble» means a small, thin stream of liquid or spit.
fact: In basketball, «double dribble» is a rule break: you cannot start dribbling again once you have stopped.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «Everyone picks their nose or dribbles in their sleep sometimes.» What does dribbles mean here? | Moves very quickly | * Lets spit run slowly out of the mouth | Kicks a ball in a game | Falls in drops from a tap
exercise: In PE, pupils practise dribbling a football through cones. What does dribbling mean in this sentence? | Standing still and waiting for the whistle | * Moving the ball along with many small kicks | Carrying a bag of kit to the field | Dripping water onto the grass
exercise: A tap may dribble when the water pressure is low. What does dribble mean here? | Shout loudly | Bounce off the wall | Run around a track | * Fall or flow slowly in drops or a thin stream
test: «Park a car under a lime tree in July and come back to a windscreen covered in sticky drops: the aphids up in the leaves have been dribbling sweet honeydew all afternoon.» Which meaning of dribbling fits best? | Shouting at the aphids | Dancing on the spot | * Falling slowly in small drops | Kicking a ball through cones
test: «You can't run while holding the ball (travelling), and once you stop dribbling you can't start again (double dribble); the ball goes to the other team.» What is a double dribble? | A team with two captains | A ball that bounces twice in a row | A player who runs with the ball in their hands | * Starting to dribble again after you have stopped, which breaks the rule
exam: In the phrase a dribble of gravy on a plate, what does dribble mean? | A football move | A large puddle | A loud noise | * A small, thin stream of liquid
